{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/593266-how-wraith-see-world-of-quick","id":593266,"title":"How Wraith See World of Quick","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Wraith: The Oblivion"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-07-16T16:39:43-07:00","id":593266,"title":"How Wraith See World of Quick","content":"I read core rule book, I know Wraiths see living world but how. Can they see all people or just see narrow amount of people who share same passion wtih them. While they are walking in shadowlands (not tempest) can they see and interact all things or not.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6954-beyefendikus","name":"Beyefendikus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-07-16T16:57:43-07:00","id":593361,"title":"","content":"Any wraith in the Shadowlands - even a &quot;newbie&quot; - can see into the Skinlands (a.k.a the living world), just by thinking about it. However, it looks &quot;wrong&quot; to them. They see the marks of Oblivion everywhere - the first traces of sickness, that small bit of rust . . . any sign of decay is immediately (and glaringly) obvious to them. But yeah, they can see everything - wraiths make great spies, in that sense.<br /> <br /> They can't actually <i>do</i> anything to Skinlands people/items, though. Barring specialized Arcanoi (wraithly powers), all they can do is watch. Their words are lost on the wind, their touch no more substantial than fog . . . wraiths are trapped on the wrong side of the Shroud, no matter how much they wish otherwise. That's part of what drives them to study forbidden Arcanoi like Embody or Possession, or to try to Rise . . . its their only hope, their one last chance to touch their loved ones (or crush their living enemies).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4624-one-vorlon","name":"One Vorlon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4624_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"537"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-07-17T04:45:29-07:00","id":593720,"title":"","content":"Wraiths also have Lifesight as well as Deathsight. They don't just see the decay in the skinlands, they see the lfie too. Living people usually look more vibrant than anything in the Shadowlands, and Wraiths can usually see their auras as well. People may be covered in deathmarks, but they are still brimming with life and with the emotions and passions Wraiths are so hungry for.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":758,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","user":"Ramnesis","date":". 07-17-2015, 07:50 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-07-17T05:56:24-07:00","id":593734,"title":"","content":"Thank you for your answers. Actually I read corerulebok two times but still sometimes i have problem for understanding wraith daily life in shadowlands. Your answers make me little more sure.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6954-beyefendikus","name":"Beyefendikus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-07-17T12:13:38-07:00","id":594040,"title":"","content":"My impression was always that the Shadowlands <i>were</i> how wraiths saw the world of the living, viewed through the filter of death. They see all the people, places, and things a living person would, but as insubstantial phantoms whose decay is readily apparent. Whereas destroyed buildings and the dead are present and solid to them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3505-matt-the-bruins-fan","name":"Matt the Bruins fan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3505_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2401"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-07-19T09:39:13-07:00","id":595708,"title":"","content":"*nods* I play it that the Shadowlands is a part of reality, just on the other side of the mirror so to speak. That it shares the space of the living world, though the lands of the dead are filled with the memories of those things not around anymore as the only things the wraiths can physically touch and interact with. The Skinlands are still around them and they walk the same streets and such as the Quick, though the wraiths pass through the stuff of the Quick and get made incorporeal by it.<br /> <br /> For example there is a house of someone in the Quick, it also is placed in the same location as the Shadowlands, though only the things broken and forgotten about are able to be touched and are still around for a wraith, who was possibly the previous occupant and the house is their Haunt. The wraith and the current living occupant both live in the same house, though the Shroud means they cannot interact properly, though of course a weakening come nighttime and certain Arcanos can make things happen, if the wraith doesn't mind breaking Charon's Law.<br /> <br /> Only difference to me between the Skinlands and the Shadowlands is roads, railways or bodies of water in the latter can sometimes eventually become Byways that lead down into the Tempest. I tend to invision this as a road out of town eventually leading in a certain place to a Nihil like portal, usually this road is downhill or leading into a dark tunnel or some other shadowed or downwards bit of a journey.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3951-wander","name":"wander","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"409"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-07-20T06:06:40-07:00","id":596799,"title":"","content":"I tend to like the imagery of byways just sort of slipping out of the Shadowlands and into the Tempest. A river that doesn't show up on any map, a road you turn right on three times and you find the city slipping away. You'd still call the transition area a Nihil, it just becomes something you could accidentally go through without knowing it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-10T13:18:07-07:00","id":622598,"title":"","content":"One thing that still puzzles me about it, and I was hoping someone could either enlighten me or confirm my suspicions. When a Wraith &quot;peeks&quot; into the Skinlands, they are incorporeal to everything there. They can walk through people, walls, furniture, animals, etc. Does this mean they are no longer in the Shadowlands? Do other Wraiths that are in the Shadowlands become hard to see, perhaps all fuzzy and ghost-like, while the world of the living is more clearly in view? If so, does that mean when Ned (a Wraith of mine) goes into the Skinlands but is still very much a non-solid ghost, drops a point of temporary corpus to become insubstantial, walks through several walls, enters and exits buildings this way and winds up on a different street, then at that point slips back into the Shadowlands.... does he appear wherever the Shadowland equivalent is or does he reappear wherever he last was within the Shadowlands?<br /> <br /> I know, kind of dizzying. WtO has a lot of concepts that take a while to wrap your head around.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7234-the-dreamweaver","name":"The Dreamweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7234_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"358"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-11T05:07:30-07:00","id":623221,"title":"","content":"The Shadowlands and the Skinlands are not really separate places like that. A Wraith in the Shadowlands already does see the Skinlands, and already has to discorporate to pass through Skinland walls. That's one of the great torments of being a Wraith, it takes very little energy to see your loved ones and the things that are important to you, but you can only interact with them with the greatest effort (Arcanoi).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-11T16:29:49-07:00","id":623748,"title":"","content":"To answer more fully also, the Wraith stays in the Shadowlands, think of like two overlapping dimensions, where people populate the same space, though cannot interact with those of the other space. If a person could see the Shadowlands side, they'd pass right through a wraith they saw there and for the <i>most part</i>, everything is pretty much the same on each side, aside for the odd relic or Haunt over in the Shadowlands. A wraith can see Skinlands stuff just fine AFAIK, though it's sort of different. Maybe someone else can add more about that.<br /> <br /> To answer the last question, Ned doesn't leave the Shadowlands, they walk through the Skinlands building and end up on the other side, though in the Shadowlands equivalent still. As said above, you still share space with your loved ones as a wraith, though neither side can touch the other and the Shroud keeps the Quick from seeing the wraiths and Shadowland, though there are exceptions of course, such as Arcanoi usage. Phantasm, or dream walking is a great way for a wraith to interact and talk with a loved one, through their dreams perpetuating the idea of a loved one having recurring dreams of someone they lost.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3951-wander","name":"wander","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"409"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-11T17:51:27-07:00","id":623812,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/593266-how-wraith-see-world-of-quick?p=623221#post623221\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Shadowlands and the Skinlands are not really separate places like that. A Wraith in the Shadowlands already does see the Skinlands, and already has to discorporate to pass through Skinland walls. That's one of the great torments of being a Wraith, it takes very little energy to see your loved ones and the things that are important to you, but you can only interact with them with the greatest effort (Arcanoi).</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>wander</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/593266-how-wraith-see-world-of-quick?p=623748#post623748\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">To answer more fully also, the Wraith stays in the Shadowlands, think of like two overlapping dimensions, where people populate the same space, though cannot interact with those of the other space. If a person could see the Shadowlands side, they'd pass right through a wraith they saw there and for the <i>most part</i>, everything is pretty much the same on each side, aside for the odd relic or Haunt over in the Shadowlands. A wraith can see Skinlands stuff just fine AFAIK, though it's sort of different. Maybe someone else can add more about that.<br /> <br /> To answer the last question, Ned doesn't leave the Shadowlands, they walk through the Skinlands building and end up on the other side, though in the Shadowlands equivalent still. As said above, you still share space with your loved ones as a wraith, though neither side can touch the other and the Shroud keeps the Quick from seeing the wraiths and Shadowland, though there are exceptions of course, such as Arcanoi usage. Phantasm, or dream walking is a great way for a wraith to interact and talk with a loved one, through their dreams perpetuating the idea of a loved one having recurring dreams of someone they lost.</div> </div> </div> </div>So what if a Skinlands wall and a Shadowlands wall both occupy the same space? I've read that by spending one point of temporary corpus, the wraith can pass through the solid matter, <i>but</i> he can never pass through the things in the Shadowlands... I'm literally looking at a game mechanic paradox, the way I understand it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":7234,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7234-the-dreamweaver","user":"The Dreamweaver","date":". 08-11-2015, 09:30 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7234-the-dreamweaver","name":"The Dreamweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7234_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"358"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-11T20:15:54-07:00","id":623893,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Dreamweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/593266-how-wraith-see-world-of-quick?p=623812#post623812\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So what if a Skinlands wall and a Shadowlands wall both occupy the same space? I've read that by spending one point of temporary corpus, the wraith can pass through the solid matter, <i>but</i> he can never pass through the things in the Shadowlands... I'm literally looking at a game mechanic paradox, the way I understand it.</div> </div> </div> </div>(Just jumping in...)<br /> <br /> It's not a game mechanic paradox, but rather a matter of thematics and &quot;levels&quot; of dimensions: when a wraith is favoring the &quot;skinland&quot;-side of things, it's reflective in the Shadowland of the *physical* wall in question -- he can pass through it via the rules as presented/indicated. *However*, when he &quot;descends&quot; deeper into the Shadowlands of the Low Umbra, things may reflect what exists in the physical world, but can vary greatly depending on how &quot;removed&quot; the wraith/location is, dimensionally speaking, from the Dark Penumbra nearest the Skinlands. (That is, there may not be any direction/spatial change in locality, but rather a change in &quot;nearness&quot; and &quot;far-ness&quot; relative to the interacting with the physical world.) Remember: even the Shadowlands (Dark Penumbra) have gradients and degrees of relative dimensionality -- the &quot;closer&quot;/&quot;higher&quot; to the Skinlands a wraith is, the more &quot;alive&quot;/&quot;real&quot; things will seem; the &quot;further&quot;/&quot;deeper&quot; into the Low Umbra he is, the more conceptual and boarder-line abstract things get (the Robin William's movie &quot;What Dreams May Come&quot; is an <b>excellent</b> example of this concept, and highly recommended by many for understanding/conceptualizing the Low Umbra). The &quot;walls&quot; that a wraith wouldn't readily be able to pass through are the ones in Stygia, deeper into the Low Umbra (though still within the broad reach of the Shadowlands/Dark Penumbra, as Stygia isn't as deep as, say, Duat or other &quot;deeper/farther&quot; realms, The Far Shores, et al). In the relatively rare instance wherein a physical wall(or what-have-you) and a near-Skinland &quot;stygian&quot; wall existed in the same spot and nearly the same &quot;level&quot; of dimensionality, it would be a cue to the wraith in question that someone has been mucking about in the place he's at (solidifying &quot;reality&quot; in a given place, 'porting in Stygian &quot;materials&quot; to the near-Skinland Dark Penumbra, or some sort of &quot;witchy-pooh&quot; is at work). Regardless of the reason, he'd have a pretty good idea that something's up because even though he can normally pass through a physical wall, he *wouldn't* be able to pass through the co-existing stygian wall (there is not paradox in this, at all -- it'd be the same as me being normally able to pass through air, but unable to pass through *that* section of air because of a static forcefield... same conceptual idea).<br /> <br /> Hope that helps!<br /> <br /> (Jesus... I think this is the first time since the forum reboot that I've posted in the Wraith section... and the more I think about it, I don't think I've posted in here since, lord... maybe not in this decade. Hunh. Time flies...)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I have been around here for waaaayyyy too fucking long...","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1433-just-john-forever","name":"Just John, Forever...","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1433_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-11T20:48:29-07:00","id":623903,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Just John, Forever...</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/593266-how-wraith-see-world-of-quick?p=623893#post623893\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">(Just jumping in...)<br /> <br /> It's not a game mechanic paradox, but rather a matter of thematics and &quot;levels&quot; of dimensions: when a wraith is favoring the &quot;skinland&quot;-side of things, it's reflective in the Shadowland of the *physical* wall in question -- he can pass through it via the rules as presented/indicated. *However*, when he &quot;descends&quot; deeper into the Shadowlands of the Low Umbra, things may reflect what exists in the physical world, but can vary greatly depending on how &quot;removed&quot; the wraith/location is, dimensionally speaking, from the Dark Penumbra nearest the Skinlands. (That is, there may not be any direction/spatial change in locality, but rather a change in &quot;nearness&quot; and &quot;far-ness&quot; relative to the interacting with the physical world.) Remember: even the Shadowlands (Dark Penumbra) have gradients and degrees of relative dimensionality -- the &quot;closer&quot;/&quot;higher&quot; to the Skinlands a wraith is, the more &quot;alive&quot;/&quot;real&quot; things will seem; the &quot;further&quot;/&quot;deeper&quot; into the Low Umbra he is, the more conceptual and boarder-line abstract things get (the Robin William's movie &quot;What Dreams May Come&quot; is an <b>excellent</b> example of this concept, and highly recommended by many for understanding/conceptualizing the Low Umbra). The &quot;walls&quot; that a wraith wouldn't readily be able to pass through are the ones in Stygia, deeper into the Low Umbra (though still within the broad reach of the Shadowlands/Dark Penumbra, as Stygia isn't as deep as, say, Duat or other &quot;deeper/farther&quot; realms, The Far Shores, et al). In the relatively rare instance wherein a physical wall(or what-have-you) and a near-Skinland &quot;stygian&quot; wall existed in the same spot and nearly the same &quot;level&quot; of dimensionality, it would be a cue to the wraith in question that someone has been mucking about in the place he's at (solidifying &quot;reality&quot; in a given place, 'porting in Stygian &quot;materials&quot; to the near-Skinland Dark Penumbra, or some sort of &quot;witchy-pooh&quot; is at work). Regardless of the reason, he'd have a pretty good idea that something's up because even though he can normally pass through a physical wall, he *wouldn't* be able to pass through the co-existing stygian wall (there is not paradox in this, at all -- it'd be the same as me being normally able to pass through air, but unable to pass through *that* section of air because of a static forcefield... same conceptual idea).<br /> <br /> Hope that helps!<br /> <br /> (Jesus... I think this is the first time since the forum reboot that I've posted in the Wraith section... and the more I think about it, I don't think I've posted in here since, lord... maybe not in this decade. Hunh. Time flies...)</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Phew! That was a big chunk of text to read, but I think I get you. So basic Shadowlands basically operate under the law of the Skinlands &quot;rule of ouch&quot; I've heard about, so long as we're not talking about those more abstract, lower levels of the underworld? I can handle that concept okay. Is this covered in the books? I don't own any myself and can only glean information through Wikis, players, and scans I locate online.<br /> <br /> Bonus question: Does that mean that a Wraith could, say, grab onto a moving train's handrails so that they could hitch a ride on a Skinlands train? Or &quot;sit&quot; in a car, since they have to make themselves insubstantial in order to pass through solid matter? I'm picturing scenes from movies like Ghost, where the main character, Sam, did this multiple times so that he could track his killer to his apartment to spy on him.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":7234,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7234-the-dreamweaver","user":"The Dreamweaver","date":". 08-11-2015, 11:51 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7234-the-dreamweaver","name":"The Dreamweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7234_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"358"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-12T04:30:34-07:00","id":624073,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Dreamweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/593266-how-wraith-see-world-of-quick?p=623903#post623903\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> Phew! That was a big chunk of text to read, but I think I get you. So basic Shadowlands basically operate under the law of the Skinlands &amp;quot;rule of ouch&amp;quot; I've heard about, so long as we're not talking about those more abstract, lower levels of the underworld? I can handle that concept okay. Is this covered in the books? I don't own any myself and can only glean information through Wikis, players, and scans I locate online.<br /> <br /> Bonus question: Does that mean that a Wraith could, say, grab onto a moving train's handrails so that they could hitch a ride on a Skinlands train? Or &amp;quot;sit&amp;quot; in a car, since they have to make themselves insubstantial in order to pass through solid matter? I'm picturing scenes from movies like Ghost, where the main character, Sam, did this multiple times so that he could track his killer to his apartment to spy on him.</div> </div> </div> </div>Walls that exist in the Skinlands also exist in the Shadowlands, but as echoes of themselves. So a wraith can walk through them as per the rule of ouch. <br /> <br /> However, a wall that only exists in the Shadowlands is solid to a wraith, and they must walk around it or find a door. Such walls were either destroyed, and so crossed over (think of this wall as the ghost of the original wall and thus as solid as a wraith, because they're made of similar stuff), or were fashioned from things in the Shadowlands to begin with (usually the corpora of wraiths, so again made of similar stuff to a wraith and so solid).<br /> <br /> In Stygia and the Tempest, everything is either made there or crossed over there, so is solid to a wraith.<br /> <br /> As per Ghost, a wraith could jump onto a moving train in the Skinlands, but would go incorporeal. She couldn't hold onto that Skinlands train, however, without one or more Arcanoi. The train isn't made of the same stuff as she is. She can walk through it, but can't touch or interact with it without Arcanoi.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer, publisher, performer<br /> Mostly he/his, sometimes she/her IRL <a href=\"https://adam-lowe.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://adam-lowe.com</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5291-adambeyoncelowe","name":"adambeyoncelowe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-12T07:07:11-07:00","id":624244,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>adambeyoncelowe</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/593266-how-wraith-see-world-of-quick?p=624073#post624073\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Walls that exist in the Skinlands also exist in the Shadowlands, but as echoes of themselves. So a wraith can walk through them as per the rule of ouch.<br /> <br /> However, a wall that only exists in the Shadowlands is solid to a wraith, and they must walk around it or find a door. Such walls were either destroyed, and so crossed over (think of this wall as the ghost of the original wall and thus as solid as a wraith, because they're made of similar stuff), or were fashioned from things in the Shadowlands to begin with (usually the corpora of wraiths, so again made of similar stuff to a wraith and so solid).<br /> <br /> In Stygia and the Tempest, everything is either made there or crossed over there, so is solid to a wraith.<br /> <br /> As per Ghost, a wraith could jump onto a moving train in the Skinlands, but would go incorporeal. She couldn't hold onto that Skinlands train, however, without one or more Arcanoi. The train isn't made of the same stuff as she is. She can walk through it, but can't touch or interact with it without Arcanoi.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Arcanoi such as Outrage to grab any and all physical objects? Also, thank you to everyone that answered my questions. They're extremely helpful and this is a fun subject to discuss as well.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7234-the-dreamweaver","name":"The Dreamweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7234_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"358"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-12T10:30:27-07:00","id":624464,"title":"","content":"A wraith could also Skinride a person as they get onto the train, which would be my method of entering it with my current character. They can then exit the person when they get off at whatever stop they happen to. You'd need a higher level of Puppetry to make the person decide what stop your wraith needs to get off though.<br /> <br /> I see the Shadowlands as a place where a wraith is generally fine to walk through things incorporeally... Venturing down into the Tempest through a Nihil will bring them to a realm they'll always be completely corporeal however. And as mentioned before, other wraiths along with Stygian and Soulsteel is very solid to a wraith, along with relics and Artifacts, basically stuff thats been destroyed and shifted over to be a Shadowland construct or things created over on that side or otherwise at home there.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3951-wander","name":"wander","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"409"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-13T04:50:37-07:00","id":625304,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Dreamweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/593266-how-wraith-see-world-of-quick?p=623903#post623903\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Bonus question: Does that mean that a Wraith could, say, grab onto a moving train's handrails so that they could hitch a ride on a Skinlands train? Or &quot;sit&quot; in a car, since they have to make themselves insubstantial in order to pass through solid matter? I'm picturing scenes from movies like Ghost, where the main character, Sam, did this multiple times so that he could track his killer to his apartment to spy on him.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't think there's any problem with just boarding a train when its stationary. Clever use of Arcanoi should also work. But as for jumping through the side and landing on the floor, I don't remember how quickly Wraiths can recorporate, or if they can selectively pass through things. That said, I'm inclined to say that under normal circumstances Wraiths invoke the Hollywood rule of phasing, which is to say just because they are intangible doesn't mean they pass through the floor if they don't want to.* <br /> <br /> I'm now imagining a fight between two Wraiths where they try to throw each other into the side of the train hard enough to make them discorporate and fall through.<br /> *Otherwise Wraiths and multi-story buildings would not mix.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-13T05:02:44-07:00","id":625306,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>wander</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/593266-how-wraith-see-world-of-quick?p=624464#post624464\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I see the Shadowlands as a place where a wraith is generally fine to walk through things incorporeally... Venturing down into the Tempest through a Nihil will bring them to a realm they'll always be completely corporeal however. And as mentioned before, other wraiths along with Stygian and Soulsteel is very solid to a wraith, along with relics and Artifacts, basically stuff thats been destroyed and shifted over to be a Shadowland construct or things created over on that side or otherwise at home there.</div> </div> </div> </div>And Wraiths can and do use said things to construct shelters and fortresses in their Necropoli.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-13T12:39:47-07:00","id":625749,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/593266-how-wraith-see-world-of-quick?p=625304#post625304\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I don't think there's any problem with just boarding a train when its stationary. Clever use of Arcanoi should also work. But as for jumping through the side and landing on the floor, I don't remember how quickly Wraiths can recorporate, or if they can selectively pass through things. That said, I'm inclined to say that under normal circumstances Wraiths invoke the Hollywood rule of phasing, which is to say just because they are intangible doesn't mean they pass through the floor if they don't want to.*<br /> <br /> I'm now imagining a fight between two Wraiths where they try to throw each other into the side of the train hard enough to make them discorporate and fall through.<br /> *Otherwise Wraiths and multi-story buildings would not mix.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> I had a funny moment in an RP yesterday when a Wraith named Carlos used Outrage. He was trailing a monster that was tearing through the streets (long story) and was attempting to subdue it before it hurt someone he cared about. He phased through things and became incorporeal, and while I didn't know if he should be able to &quot;make his feet solid&quot; rapidly or not, I simply played it like he was rushing himself so fast that he goofed. So he phased through walls, then attempted to use the basic ability of Outrage (Leap of Rage) to jump to the top of a building... He then realized his error, cursed, and fell straight through several floors back to ground level before becoming solid(ish) again.<br /> <br /> It does raise another important question: If it means a Wraith would fall through floors, what is stopping them from just falling through the ground into a sewer system, or down even lower than that? Me, personally, I feel it should be an intuitive but concentration thing. A Wraith completely stressed out, or perhaps low on Corpus might find it harder to keep from falling through things because their feet aren't &quot;sticking&quot; to things too well in those emotional/metaphysical conditions. This is of course providing that they are still incorporeal at a moment before of the whole Rule of Ouch.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":7234,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7234-the-dreamweaver","user":"The Dreamweaver","date":". 08-13-2015, 03:46 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7234-the-dreamweaver","name":"The Dreamweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7234_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"358"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-13T14:57:47-07:00","id":625959,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Dreamweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/593266-how-wraith-see-world-of-quick?p=625749#post625749\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> I had a funny moment in an RP yesterday when a Wraith named Carlos used Outrage. He was trailing a monster that was tearing through the streets (long story) and was attempting to subdue it before it hurt someone he cared about. He phased through things and became incorporeal, and while I didn't know if he should be able to &quot;make his feet solid&quot; rapidly or not, I simply played it like he was rushing himself so fast that he goofed. So he phased through walls, then attempted to use the basic ability of Outrage (Leap of Rage) to jump to the top of a building... He then realized his error, cursed, and fell straight through several floors back to ground level before becoming solid(ish) again.</div> </div> </div> </div>I love it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It does raise another important question: If it means a Wraith would fall through floors, what is stopping them from just falling through the ground into a sewer system, or down even lower than that? Me, personally, I feel it should be an intuitive but concentration thing. A Wraith completely stressed out, or perhaps low on Corpus might find it harder to keep from falling through things because their feet aren't &quot;sticking&quot; to things too well in those emotional/metaphysical conditions. This is of course providing that they are still incorporeal at a moment before of the whole Rule of Ouch. </div> </div> </div>Precisely why I would usually invoke Hollywood on this one, because the alternative makes it too easy to derail a campaign. However I do like stressful situations or low corpus making it possible for a Wraith to trip, fall, or drop a story if it is dramatically appropriate. Especially on young Wraiths or Wraiths who spend most of their time below the Shadowlands.<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-15T13:14:54-07:00","id":627815,"title":"","content":"Another question popped into my mind just a few moments ago. Let's say a Wraith has Outrage (3-5) and also has a relic fire axe or some other object that can be used as a weapon to smash or chop. Can their Wraithly powers extend to the relic objects and attack objects/people across the Shroud, or would their relic axe/bat/club simply pass through the Quick?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7234-the-dreamweaver","name":"The Dreamweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7234_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"358"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-19T09:30:52-07:00","id":632390,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/593266-how-wraith-see-world-of-quick?p=625304#post625304\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I don't think there's any problem with just boarding a train when its stationary. Clever use of Arcanoi should also work. But as for jumping through the side and landing on the floor, I don't remember how quickly Wraiths can recorporate, or if they can selectively pass through things. That said, I'm inclined to say that under normal circumstances Wraiths invoke the Hollywood rule of phasing, which is to say just because they are intangible doesn't mean they pass through the floor if they don't want to.*<br /> <br /> I'm now imagining a fight between two Wraiths where they try to throw each other into the side of the train hard enough to make them discorporate and fall through.<br /> *Otherwise Wraiths and multi-story buildings would not mix.</div> </div> </div> </div>Rules say you stay incorporeal for (Stamina) rounds, but I'm generally liberal when it comes to selective incorporeality during that time -- and as you folks point out, there's a lot of support for that in the canon (WW-published and otherwise). After experimenting with writing up my own Arcanos: Insubstantiality and having people make Stamina + arcanos rating checks to do various things, I decided it was too much complication. I pretty much just allow things if I can't think of a reason why not. It makes the game more fun.<br /> <br /> Guidelines I fall back on are: Shadowland physics (and for that matter, arcanoi and all WW magic) result from shared belief. It takes an act of will, with a cost, to pass through a wall because it doesn't feel natural based on life experience.* You can treat floors the same way, separately from any wall someone might be passing through at the time. And by the same token, I would let a wraith (or spectre) pick you up, spin you around until you're disoriented, then throw you through the floor.** My house rule: you always stop when you get to a sold dirt/stone/concrete barrier more than a couple of meters thick (that is, about 6 feet thick in the direction you're moving).<br /> <br /> I'm also pretty liberal with most &quot;can arcanos X do this?&quot; sorts of questions:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Dreamweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/593266-how-wraith-see-world-of-quick?p=627815#post627815\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Another question popped into my mind just a few moments ago. Let's say a Wraith has Outrage (3-5) and also has a relic fire axe or some other object that can be used as a weapon to smash or chop. Can their Wraithly powers extend to the relic objects and attack objects/people across the Shroud, or would their relic axe/bat/club simply pass through the Quick?</div> </div> </div> </div>I would say it can, but treat damage dealt according to standard Outrage rules. So technically a karate chop is just as effective -- but aesthetically, I like the axe better and would encourage that kind of thing.<br /> <br /> * - If a wraith has been dead a long time I might let them get away with more since death is normal to them, but you don't want to go too far in this direction because the setting is weird enough already. Most such wraiths are either drones, or too important for the PCs to have much contact with.<br /> <br /> ** - And/or jump up from beneath the floor to attack you, then drop back through it and latch onto the rafters below. Whatever we find amusing/terrifying.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4448-bphillips","name":"bphillips","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4448_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"30"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}